From d0b4b710ee4d18a00da860da7bf921051a224ba8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: RK3128 reference <reference@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 10:35:53 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] soc: rockchip: Add an arbiter for the RK3128 HEVC/VPU
 shared bus

On RK3128 the HEVC decoder (driven by rkvdec) and the Hantro VPU have no
bus clocks of their own: both run on ACLK_VDPU/HCLK_VDPU, and
GRF_SOC_CON1 bit 15 (hevc_vpu_sel, reset value 0 = VPU) connects one of
them to the shared bus interface. Rockchip's kernels only touch a block
and its IOMMU while the bit points at it: the IOMMU of the block that
loses the bus is disabled before the switch, the IOMMU of the block that
gets it is enabled after (vcodec_service vcodec_enter_mode(), MPP
vdpu_3036_set_grf()).

Mainline has two independent drivers for the two halves, and the IOMMUs
are also reached from outside them (driver core resumes an IOMMU for its
consumer's probe and unbind, and the DMA layer attaches its domain then).
So arbitrate where every register access is already gated: runtime PM.

The two decoders and their IOMMUs are clients. Each driver calls
power_on() first in its runtime resume callback and power_off() last in
its runtime suspend callback, and touches registers only inside those
callbacks or while RPM_ACTIVE. The arbiter writes the bit, under its
lock, to a side only when no client of the other side is powered on, and
back to the VPU when the last HEVC client powers off. So a block or an
IOMMU is only touched while the bit points at it, whatever path resumed
it.

A side that wants the bus waits (3 s at most, then -EBUSY, which fails
the resume without a sticky runtime PM error) for the other side to
power off, and asks the other side's devices to runtime-suspend now
instead of after their autosuspend delay. That is only a hint: a device
that stays active keeps the bus for its side.

Roles come from the devicetree (the decoders by compatible, an IOMMU
through a decoder's "iommus"), so an IOMMU is a client from its own
probe on, before any consumer binds. The GRF is found by compatible,
with the register, bit and decoder compatibles as match data, so no
binding needs a GRF phandle.

Signed-off-by: RK3128 reference <reference@example.invalid>
